se cells are very special so they  can be  differentiated into virtually any type  of cell within the human body  and they have the ability to self-renew  so these cells can be derived from from  two main sources  we have the embryonic stem cells which  are derived from the inner cell mass of  the blastocyst  and we have the induced pluripotent stem  cells or ips cells  these cells are reaper are somatic cells  that are reprogrammed back to a  pluripotent stem state  like so the main advantage of using  ips cells when comparing with embryonic  stem cells  is that we would not have the ethical  concerns that are  often associated with embryonic stem  cells  due to the destruction of a potential  human life  also ips cells give us the ability  to do disease modeling in vitro as they  can derive patient-specific cells  and then culture them in the lab to see  how the disease progresses  we can also use them for drug-istic  efficacy testing  for drug safety and in the future we  hope to use them for  organ replacement therapy but before we  can do any of this  we still need to have large numbers of  cells  and we need to obtain pure populations  of the cells that we desire  and in order to do this in vitro we have  three main platforms that are used  for the culture of the cells the first  one and the most classical approach  is the monolayer where we have cells  attached to a surface which is coated  with an extracellular matrix  so this system provides a defined  substrate for cellular attachment  and allows a uniform application of  external stimuli however since the  surface area available may be  too low we would we would end up with  low cell yields and this can be  surpassed by using microcarriers  so microcarriers are small particles  that can be coated with ecm  that provide us scalability by providing  large surface areas for cells to adhere  to  we may have some troubles in here  because the cells may have low addition  to the microcarriers  and there isn't a high cost associated  since for using the cells  we would end up with higher costs in the  downstream processing because we need to  separate the cells from the micro  carriers in order to be able to use them  and to surpass this we have steroids or  3d  aggregates which are the ones that i  work with  so in here instead of having the cells  attached to a surface  the cells are actually attached to each  other  providing a 3d structure and  like this we are able to better mimic  the individual environment  and since we are not dependent on the  surface  that cells need to adhere to we may have  high cell yields comparing with the  other systems  and as we do not have to use micro  carriers or  icm the process ends up being more cost  efficient however we need to  take in mind uh that may be some size  limitations  because we need to consider that if the  aggregates get  too big which is around more than 300 to  400 micrometers in diameter  there may be necrotic centers  in the aggregates where the gas transfer  and the nutrients  do not reach so we need to take care of  that  and the great thing about the cells is  that in vitro  we can virtually derive the cells into  any type of cell within the human body  and to do this we modulate the signaling  pathways  to to to direct cells to the lineage  that we desire so we can end up with  cardiomyocytes  with neuroprogenitors or even insulin  producing cells  in this case i'm going to try and force  myself to go  towards the neural progenitor state  as i mentioned so i used aggregates to  to do this process and free free  floating aggregates  uh often have one another problem is  that  they tend to agglomerate if they are not  under the right conditions and  in order to prevent this we can use  micro wealth  so in this microwave we are preventing  the aggregate from agglomerating  uh at the same time we can also um  define a specific cell number that we  want to have in each aggregate and it  has also been demonstrated 